# REGRESSION (graduated from `known_gaps/`) — the minimal repro of the
# container-literal element-type mint, kept at the exact shape it was filed
# with.
#
# AXIS CELL: array literal × `String` element × `.get(i).unwrap()` borrow source.
# ONE cell. The axis-complete nets are `container_literal_borrow_elem_array.gg`
# (9 type cells), `..._dict.gg` (key and value) and `..._set.gg` (both key
# channels, ordered and unordered).
#
# THE DEFECT: the literal minted its destination element type from the source
# operand BEFORE that operand was materialized, so a borrow-typed source minted
# `Vector[Ptr(T)]` — 8-byte slots with no element drop/clone/materialize vtable.
# The clone WAS emitted correctly; it was the SLOT that was wrong, which is why
# the ownership machinery looked innocent.
#
# RED pre-fix, on BOTH backends: prints `1` and then an EMPTY line. `gg check`
# returned "OK: no semantic errors" and both backends exited 0 — while ggdef
# printed the correct `bb`. Core #8: one accepted program, three lanes, the
# oracle right and both production backends wrong.
#
# WHY A GREEN SUITE CARRIED IT FOR SO LONG: `Ptr(T)` is not `is_resource_type`,
# so the bogus element type laundered the value straight past the fatal
# `ShallowCopyOfResource` validator — the guard that exists for exactly this
# class could not see it. Under ASan the String cell aborts
# (allocation-size-too-big, `0xbe` freed-poison read as a length), but the `int`
# cell is ASan-CLEAN and reads back a live address, so the sanitizer is blind to
# half the class and ggdef is the only instrument that adjudicates it.
#
# No for-loop, no `&` sigil, no patch. Four lines of ordinary syntax.

Vector[String] mk():
    Vector[String] v = ["aa", "bb"]
    auto s = v.get(1).unwrap()
    Vector[String] out = [s]
    return out

void main():
    Vector[String] r = mk()
    print(r.len())
    print(r.get(0).unwrap())
